  "narrative": "No assessments are due in this explore stage, so I did not change existing claim assessments. Returned estimates and intervals will already be signed to the registered hypothesis direction and must not be multiplied again. For PTEN-loss claims with direction -1, a positive signed estimate means lower raw dependency_composite, i.e. stronger composite dependency; a negative signed estimate means weaker or opposite evidence. Threshold acceptance requires a signed estimate above +0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction with credible interval support and adequate cell counts.\n\nThe most important new accepted predictor is H93: PTEN loss in a dominant-loss-free background excluding TP53, SMARCA4, and KEAP1 losses. This was supported by discovery R131 +0.252 [0.169,0.335] and independent validation R132 +0.260 [0.132,0.389]. Therefore, this explore stage focuses on scientifically useful PTEN follow-up rather than broad additional fishing. I registered two lineage-context mean effects within the same dominant-loss-free background: PTEN loss in adenocarcinoma and in squamous. These test whether the accepted cleaner-background PTEN association is present in each major lineage subtype. Expected direction is the accepted stronger-dependency direction, but a null result would only show a context-specific absence or weaker effect, not refute H93.\n\nI also registered a lower-Cas9-activity PTEN mean effect within the dominant-loss-free background. High-Cas9 PTEN context is already registered as H198; low-Cas9 activity completes a useful technical robustness profile, especially because previous high-Cas9 attenuation signals for SMARCA4 and KEAP1 did not consistently survive validation. A threshold-supported low-Cas9 PTEN effect would indicate the association is not confined to high-Cas9 measurements; a null or attenuated result would be a context-specific observation rather than a refutation of H93.\n\nI registered an overall PTEN-loss effect in the lower screen-quality stratum screen_roc_auc <= 0.95. This complements the accepted high-screen-quality contexts for the dominant predictors and the registered clean-background screen-quality tests. Because this proposal is not restricted to the dominant-loss-free background, a positive result would show a lower-screen-quality overall PTEN association but could still be influenced by co-occurring TP53, SMARCA4, KEAP1, or other alterations. A null result would indicate quality- or background-dependent PTEN effects, not necessarily invalidate H93.\n\nFinally, I registered a PIK3CA-mutation interaction for PTEN loss. PIK3CA activating mutation and PTEN loss both affect the PI3K/AKT pathway, so their effects may be redundant or synergistic. The expected direction is intentionally uncertain. For direction -1, a positive signed interaction means the PTEN-loss stronger-dependency association is larger in PIK3CA-mutant samples than in the PIK3CA-wild-type complement; a negative signed interaction means attenuation or reversal. This is a biologically motivated but still dataset-level co-alteration test. All conclusions remain dataset-level associations, not causal claims or generalizable biological mechanisms."
